Installation instructions for Eigen, being a plain Account of how the Library
may be got into the House
***********************************

Explanation before starting, in which the Reader is reassured
*************************************************************

Eigen consists only of header files, hence there is nothing to compile
before you can use it; the traveller who arrives expecting a long and
laborious journey by the stage-coach of the build system will find, to his
considerable astonishment, that he has already arrived. Moreover, these header
files do not depend on your platform, they are the same for everybody, as the
sun, the law, and the weather in an English November are the same for all.

Method 1. Installing without using CMake, as the plain man does it
****************************************************************************

You can use right away the headers in the Eigen/ subdirectory. In order
to install, just copy this Eigen/ subdirectory to your favorite location,
wherever that spot may be, and whatever sentiment may have attached it to your
affections. If you also want the contrib features, copy the contrib/
subdirectory too, that it may keep its elder brother company.

Method 2. Installing using CMake, as the methodical man does it
***************************************************************

Let's call this directory 'source_dir' (where this INSTALL file is, and where,
at this very moment, you are doing the Reader the honor of looking at it).
Before starting, create another directory which we will call 'build_dir'.

Do:

  cd build_dir
  cmake source_dir
  make install

The "make install" step may require administrator privileges; for it is a
rule of the world, and not of Eigen, that a gentleman who would set up house in
the best quarters must first present his credentials to the person at the door.

You can adjust the installation destination (the "prefix")
by passing the -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=myprefix option to cmake, as is
explained in the message that cmake prints at the end, with the air of a
host who, having seen his guests comfortably disposed, gives them a parting
word upon the subject of the cellar.
